# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the portable MeshCore core.
#
# ONE vendored file, TWO constructors, and nothing else. Every other leak,
# in our code or in any other part of the vendored trees, is still reported
# and still fails the sanitised run.
#
# vendor/RIFT src/helpers/StaticPoolPacketManager.cpp:
#
#   PacketQueue::PacketQueue(int max_entries) {
#     _table = new mesh::Packet*[max_entries];
#     _pri_table = new uint8_t[max_entries];
#     _schedule_table = new uint32_t[max_entries];
#     ...
#
#   StaticPoolPacketManager::StaticPoolPacketManager(int pool_size) : ... {
#     for (int i = 0; i < pool_size; i++) unused.add(new mesh::Packet(), 0, 0);
#
# Neither class declares a destructor, so the three arrays per queue and the
# pool of Packets are never freed. On the devices MeshCore was written for
# that is deliberate rather than careless: the packet manager is constructed
# once at boot, from a fixed pool size, and lives until the device is
# powered off. Freeing it would be dead code on an MCU.
#
# It is not a defect this port should paper over in the source, because
# "fix somebody else's allocation lifetime" is exactly the kind of rewrite
# P1A is meant not to do, and changing a vendored file to quiet a sanitizer
# is the worst version of it. A MeshCore service will construct one manager
# at start-up and keep it for the life of the process, so the leak is
# bounded by the pool size and never grows - the same shape as on a device.
#
# What makes it visible here at all is only that the tests construct several
# managers in one process and then exit.
#
# Recorded as hardening debt in README.md. If MeshCore ever gains a service
# that recycles packet managers at runtime, this suppression must go and the
# destructors must be contributed upstream.
leak:PacketQueue::PacketQueue
leak:StaticPoolPacketManager::StaticPoolPacketManager
